00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:58.460
Why This Works: The Mathematical Impossibility of Committees.

221
00:23:59.640 --> 00:23:59.720
About two hundred F.D.A. bureaucrats decide what eight billion people can try when dying.

222
00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:05.040
Here's the math on why that can't work: The F.D.A. approves about fifty new drugs per year.

223
00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:12.640
There are ten thousand known diseases. At that rate, covering every disease once takes two hundred years.

224
00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:19.480
And that's before you account for the fact that different patients respond differently to the same drug based on genetics,

225
00:24:19.900 --> 00:24:26.760
age, comorbidities, and whether they had breakfast. The real bottleneck isn't speed; it's information.

226
00:24:27.440 --> 00:24:34.140
Centralized approval assumes a two hundred-person committee can evaluate what works for eight billion genetically unique

227
00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:43.440
humans. They can't. A decentralized system where millions of patients generate real-world evidence simultaneously processes

228
00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:48.620
more therapeutic information in a month than the F.D.A. generates in a decade.

229
00:24:49.540 --> 00:24:53.940
The F.D.A. doesn't know: What disease YOU have (they haven't met you).

230
00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:58.060
How YOUR body responds to treatments (genetics are unique).

231
00:24:58.780 --> 00:25:03.820
What risks YOU'RE willing to take (some prefer death to side effects, others the reverse).

232
00:25:04.460 --> 00:25:08.700
Whether YOU'D rather die trying or die waiting (only you can answer this).

233
00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:15.730
But they decide for you anyway. This isn't ideology. It's information theory.

234
00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:21.440
You mathematically cannot centralize medical decisions for eight billion unique people.

235
00:25:22.340 --> 00:25:26.460
Every civilization I've worked with tried a centralized approval committee first.

236
00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:34.600
Every single one eventually abandoned it, once someone did the math and realized that a roomful of experts reviewing one

237
00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:41.860
drug at a time is just a queue with a death rate. The Future: Where Death Becomes Embarrassing.

